lone. [Data: Entities (1529, 1491); Relationships (2427)]\\n\\nPS is also described as reflecting a person’s general level of fitness (and general fitness is separately represented as an entity). This reinforces that PS is meant to summarize overall functional capacity, not solely disease stage or diagnosis. [Data: Entities (2456, 2975, 1491); Relationships (4366, 5648)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The PS scale is level-based, with explicit descriptors for PS 1 and PS 2–4\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Within the PS framework, PS 1 is described as being able to perform light to moderate activity with some limitations, indicating a mild functional impairment rather than complete inability to function. This connects a numerical level to practical activity tolerance. [Data: Entities (2457, 1491); Relationships (4367)]\\n\\nFor PS 2–4, the community uses chair or bed confinement as the defining limitation, emphasizing escalating impairment that affects self-care ability and time spent restricted to bed/chair. This highlights that PS levels are distinguished by degree of mobility and independence. [Data: Entities (2458, 1491); Relationships (4368)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"PS 5 is explicitly defined as death, and is represented both as a level and as a concept\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The endpoint of the ECOG PS framework is captured explicitly: PS 5 is defined as meaning the person is not alive, with DEATH (PS 5) represented as an entity and also mirrored by a separate PS 5 entity. The relationships link PS to DEATH (PS 5) and separately link PS 5 back to performance status, underscoring the explicit mapping. [Data: Entities (2459, 3870, 1491); Relationships (4369, 8079)]\\n\\nThis representation matters for interpretation: PS is not only about gradations of impairment but also includes a terminal category, which is important for consistent documentation and for understanding what the highest PS level signifies in the scale. [Data: Entities (2459, 3870); Relationships (4369, 8079)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is an alternative standardized scale for assessing PS\",\n      \"explanation\": \"KARNOFSKY PERFORMANCE STATUS (KPS) is explicitly described as another performance status scale used to rate a patient’s general health and ability to function in daily activities, serving a similar purpose to ECOG performance status scoring. The relationship indicates PS can be assessed using standardized rating scales and that KPS is one such scale providing a structured way to quantify performance status. [Data: Entities (2960, 1491); Relationships (5646)]\\n\\nThis establishes that the community includes more than one scoring framework for the same underlying construct (PS), suggesting interoperability or substitution depending on clinical context or preference. [Data: Entities (2960, 1491); Relationships (5646)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Qualitative descriptors such as “poor PS” are explicitly tied to the PS concept\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The entity POOR PS is included as a qualitative description of performance status (noted as contrasting with “good PS”), and it is directly linked back to PERFORMANCE STATUS (PS). This indicates that, alongside numeric scoring (ECOG/KPS), clinicians may also use plain-language categorization to summarize functional status. [Data: Entities (3872, 1491); Relationships (8081)]\\n\\nBecause the community does not provide additional context beyond the contrast with “good PS,” it supports only the limited conclusion that “poor PS” is an acknowledged descriptive label within PS-related clinical discourse, rather than a separate measurement instrument. [Data: Entities (3872); Relationships (8081)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 2.3,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity is low because the community describes a standard clinical assessment framework (PS/ECOG/KPS) used for treatment planning rather than activity ind